.TH DU 1 
.SH NAME
du  \-  summarize disk usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B du
[
.B \-s
] [
.B \-a
] [ name ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Du
gives the number of blocks contained in all files
and (recursively) directories within each specified directory or
file
.IR name .
If
.I name
is missing,
`\fB.\fR'
is used.
.PP
The optional argument
.B \-s
causes only the grand total to
be given.
The optional argument
.B \-a
causes an entry to be generated
for each file.
Absence of either causes an entry to be generated for
each directory only.
.PP
A file which has two links to it is only counted once.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 3072 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
a block on this machine is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- so every number
.I du
prints is one sixth of what the same tree would have shown on a PDP\-11.
There is no
.I BSHIFT
to go with it; 3072 is not a power of two.
.IR Ls (1)
counts the same block in its
.B \-s
and
.B total
output, and
.IR df (1M)
and
.IR quot (1M)
report in it too.
.PP
.B "Note:"
the count is derived from each file's
.I size
and so charges a file for the holes in it and not for its indirect blocks \- the same
approximation
.IR quot (1M)
makes.
.IR Icheck (1M)
is the program that would count blocks actually allocated, and is not ported.
.SH BUGS
Non-directories
given as arguments (not under
.B \-a
option) are not listed.
.br
If there are too many distinct linked files,
.I du
counts the excess files multiply.
.br
.B "Note:"
a path longer than 256 characters, or one grown past that by the walk, is refused with
.B "--path too long"
and skipped.
v7 appended one component per directory level into a fixed buffer with no test of any kind.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
df(1M), quot(1M), ls(1)
